test_timer — kernel timer subscription sanity test
===================================================

Subscribes to the periodic timer with `period=5` ticks, then loops
`K=10` times: `notify_wait()` → `notify_read_self()` → sanity-check
value is `2` (the timer value, distinguished from virtio=1).

Prints:
- `test_timer: subscribed idx=N`
- `test_timer: got K wakes value=2 delta=T`
- `test_timer: ok` on success, or `test_timer: FAIL ...` on mismatch.

Success criterion: received `K` wakes AND elapsed ticks ≥ `K*period`.
The time-elapsed check is what proves the kernel actually parked us
between wakes rather than returning stale `notify_pending`. If the
timer never fires during a quiet channel, `notify_wait()` blocks and
the test times out in the harness — a detectable failure mode.

    module oskit.bin.test_timer

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with, uptime, svc_timer_subscribe, notify_wait, notify_read_self}


    print_udec(v: int)
        if v >= 10
            print_udec(v / 10)
        var buf: [1]byte
        buf[0] = byte('0' + (v % 10))
        puts(string(&buf[0], 1))


    main(argv: []string)
        val period = 5
        val k = 10
        val idx = svc_timer_subscribe(period)
        if idx < 0
            puts("test_timer: subscribe failed\n")
            exit_with(1)
            return
        puts("test_timer: subscribed idx=")
        print_udec(idx)
        puts("\n")

        val t0 = uptime()
        var i = 0
        var bad_value = 0
        while i < k
            notify_wait()
            val v = notify_read_self()
            if v != 2
                bad_value = v
            i += 1
        val t1 = uptime()
        val delta = t1 - t0

        puts("test_timer: got ")
        print_udec(k)
        puts(" wakes value=")
        print_udec(2)
        puts(" delta=")
        print_udec(delta)
        puts("\n")

        if bad_value != 0
            puts("test_timer: FAIL unexpected value=")
            print_udec(bad_value)
            puts("\n")
            exit_with(2)
            return
        // Minimum intrinsic time from 1st to Kth wake is (K-1)*period ticks.
        // Scheduler alignment can shave a tick off either end, so the
        // practical floor we enforce is (K-1)*period-1 — still catches
        // "kernel fired instantly without waiting" regressions. Seen on
        // aarch64: delta=48 for K=10 period=5 (theoretical 45 floor).
        val floor = (k - 1) * period - 1
        if delta < floor
            puts("test_timer: FAIL delta too short, expected >= ")
            print_udec(floor)
            puts("\n")
            exit_with(3)
            return
        puts("test_timer: ok\n")
        exit()
